1
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:14,620
Clarify something for me.

2
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:17,170
Isn't the point of a communal meal

3
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:19,670
the exchange of ideas and opinions?

4
00:00:19,670 --> 00:00:22,910
An opportunity to consider important issues of the day?

5
00:00:22,910 --> 00:00:23,890
It is.

6
00:00:23,890 --> 00:00:25,220
You just kind of put a damper on things

7
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:27,180
when you said, "The next person I see talking

8
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:29,850
with food in their mouth will be put to death."

9
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:33,160
Well, we could argue about who said what all night long,

10
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:35,960
but to set things back on course,

11
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:38,520
I will propose a new topic of conversation.

12
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:39,570
Great.

13
00:00:39,570 --> 00:00:42,790
What is the best number?

14
00:00:42,790 --> 00:00:45,990
By the way, there's only one correct answer.

15
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:50,740
5,318,008?

16
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:52,180
Wrong.

17
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:55,540
The best number is 73.

18
00:00:58,710 --> 00:01:00,030
You're probably wondering why.

19
00:01:00,030 --> 00:01:00,920
No. Uh-uh. We're good.

20
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:03,650
73 is the 21st prime number.

21
00:01:03,650 --> 00:01:07,720
Its mirror, 37, is the 12th, and its mirror, 21,

22
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:09,230
is the product of multiplying--

23
00:01:09,230 --> 00:01:12,830
hang on to your hats-- seven and three.

24
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:16,620
Eh? Eh? Did I lie?

25
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:17,860
We get it.

26
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:21,040
73 is the Chuck Norris of numbers.

27
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:23,310
Chuck Norris wishes.

28
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:26,300
In binary, 73 is a palindrome:

29
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:29,000
"one-zero-zero-one-zero-zero-one"

30
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,640
which backwards is "one-zero- zero-one-zero zero one"

31
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:33,830
exactly the same.

32
00:01:33,830 --> 00:01:37,640
All Chuck Norris backwards gets you is: "Sirron Kcuhc."

33
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:39,850
Just for the record,

34
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:43,820
when you enter 5,318,008 on a calculator,

35
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:46,870
upside-down it spells "boobies""

36
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:51,530
Remember when you were wondering

37
00:01:51,530 --> 00:01:53,150
why the girls didn't want to eat with us tonight?

38
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:54,960
Yeah, I get it now.

39
00:01:57,070 --> 00:01:58,990
I love your little heart locket, Bernadette.

40
00:01:58,990 --> 00:01:59,850
Oh, thanks.

41
00:01:59,850 --> 00:02:01,240
Howard gave it to me.

42
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:02,450
It's the cutest thing.

43
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:04,240
Every time I have dinner with his mom,

44
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:06,480
the next day I get jewelry.

45
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:09,980
Did you know that the iconic Valentine's heart shape

46
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:12,060
is not actually based on the shape of a human heart,

47
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:15,590
but rather on the shape of the buttocks of a female bending over?

48
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:19,910
Oh, so I spent seventh grade

49
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:22,830
dotting my I's with little asses?

50
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:25,020
Cool.

51
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:27,910
Hey, Penny, how's it going?

52
00:02:27,910 --> 00:02:29,350
Hey, Zack, what are you doing here?

53
00:02:29,350 --> 00:02:31,670
My dad's company prints the menus for this place.

54
00:02:31,670 --> 00:02:33,620
I'm just dropping off some new ones laminated.

55
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:36,240
Makes 'em easier to clean if people throw up on 'em.

56
00:02:37,190 --> 00:02:38,470
Guess how I got the idea?

57
00:02:38,470 --> 00:02:40,440
Yeah, I got it, I got it.

58
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:44,060
Uh, Zack, these are my friends Bernadette and Amy.

59
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:45,220
- Hi. - Hey.

60
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:46,760
Hoo.

61
00:02:49,910 --> 00:02:51,080
Okay, well, it was good to see you.

62
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:52,690
Yeah, you, too.

63
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:54,800
He's really cute. How do you know him?

64
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:56,650
Oh, we went out a couple of times.

65
00:02:56,650 --> 00:02:58,590
I'm often flummoxed by current slang.

66
00:02:58,590 --> 00:03:01,220
Does "went out" mean "had intercourse"?

67
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:04,340
- Yes. - No, no.

68
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:06,060
But in this case, yes.

69
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:07,500
Interesting.

70
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:08,900
And was it not satisfactory?

71
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:09,840
No, it was great.

72
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:13,640
He just didn't really challenge me on an intellectual level.

73
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:17,140
Couldn't you just fool around with him and then listen to NPR?

74
00:03:18,470 --> 00:03:21,020
Wouldn't help. Zack can't even spell NPR.

75
00:03:22,290 --> 00:03:23,540
It's what I do with Howard.

76
00:03:23,540 --> 00:03:25,070
I'm much smarter than he is.

77
00:03:25,070 --> 00:03:27,560
But it's important to protect his manhood.

78
00:03:29,970 --> 00:03:32,230
Hoo.

79
00:03:32,950 --> 00:03:34,210
What's the matter?

80
00:03:34,210 --> 00:03:35,760
I'm suddenly feeling flushed.

81
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:39,020
My heart rate is elevated, my palms are clammy,

82
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:40,290
my mouth is dry.

83
00:03:40,290 --> 00:03:43,450
In addition, I keep involuntarily saying "hoo."

84
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:47,490
Oh, we know what's causing that, don't we?

85
00:03:47,490 --> 00:03:48,840
It's no mystery.

86
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:49,970
I obviously have the flu

87
00:03:49,970 --> 00:03:53,030
coupled with sudden-onset Tourette's syndrome.

88
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,260
Did you hear about the accident at the bio lab?

89
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:25,380
- No. - What happened?

90
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:28,110
They were injecting rats with radioactive isotopes

91
00:04:28,110 --> 00:04:30,410
and one of the techs got bit.

92
00:04:30,410 --> 00:04:32,500
Did he get superpowers?

93
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:38,420
No, he got five stitches and a tetanus shot.

94
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:40,900
Oh. Well, that's disappointing.

95
00:04:41,450 --> 00:04:42,340
Why?

96
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:44,800
Well, you get bit by a radioactive animal in a lab,

97
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:47,280
you kind of want to turn into a superhero.

98
00:04:47,860 --> 00:04:50,890
Yeah, but who'd want to become Rat-Man?

99
00:04:51,210 --> 00:04:52,820
Who wouldn't?

100
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:56,040
You could zip through a maze in nothing flat,

101
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:57,720
squeeze through really small holes,

102
00:04:57,720 --> 00:05:00,530
and shut down restaurants in a single bound.

103
00:05:00,970 --> 00:05:03,020
And the best part is, if I were Rat-Man,

104
00:05:03,020 --> 00:05:06,360
you could be my sidekick, Mouse Boy.

105
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:12,100
Mouse Boy?

106
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:13,210
You don't like "Mouse Boy"?

107
00:05:13,210 --> 00:05:15,740
How about "Kid Vermin"?

108
00:05:16,230 --> 00:05:17,770
First of all, if we had superpowers,

109
00:05:17,770 --> 00:05:20,320
I wouldn't be the sidekick. You'd be the sidekick.

110
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:22,940
Rat-Man is nobody's sidekick.

111
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:24,750
Leonard, settle this.

112
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:27,180
Of the two of us, who's the obvious sidekick?

113
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:29,010
Yeah, Leonard, who?

114
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:35,500
12 years after high school, and I'm still at the nerd table.

115
00:05:39,210 --> 00:05:42,460
Aren't you slicing that man's brain a little too thin?

116
00:05:47,390 --> 00:05:48,620
It's too thin if I were making

117
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:51,500
a foot-long brain sandwich at Quiznos.

118
00:05:52,410 --> 00:05:56,430
For examination under a two-photon microscope, it's fine.

119
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:01,630
Well, you're the expert.

120
00:06:01,630 --> 00:06:05,770
If the correct way to do it is the wrong way, then I yield.

121
00:06:05,770 --> 00:06:06,530
Very well.

122
00:06:06,530 --> 00:06:08,240
If you die and donate your body to science,

123
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:11,520
I promise to slice your brain like Canadian bacon.

124
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:14,000
Thank you.

125
00:06:14,490 --> 00:06:15,360
Now, if you'll excuse me,

126
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,320
I have to take my temperature.

127
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:19,940
Are you monitoring your circadian rhythms

128
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:23,340
in order to identify your periods of maximum mental acuity?

129
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:24,640
I did that one summer.

130
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:26,600
Ah, youth.

131
00:06:27,890 --> 00:06:30,510
No, I experienced some distressing symptoms last night,

132
00:06:30,510 --> 00:06:33,270
so I'm checking my vital signs every hour.

133
00:06:33,270 --> 00:06:35,120
I'd be happy to create a chart

134
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:37,400
and participate in a differential diagnosis.

135
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:39,480
Oh, that sounds like fun.

136
00:06:41,510 --> 00:06:43,500
All right.

137
00:06:44,250 --> 00:06:45,780
What were the symptoms?

138
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:49,460
Elevated heart rate, moist palms, dry mouth

139
00:06:49,460 --> 00:06:52,220
and localized vascular throbbing.

140
00:06:53,710 --> 00:06:56,190
Localized to what region?

141
00:06:57,990 --> 00:07:00,430
Ears and genitalia.

142
00:07:01,660 --> 00:07:02,920
Interesting.

143
00:07:02,920 --> 00:07:05,750
Not body parts that usually team up.

144
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:10,550
What about environmental factors?

145
00:07:10,550 --> 00:07:12,610
Describe the scene for me.

146
00:07:12,610 --> 00:07:14,680
I was sitting in a restaurant with Penny and Bernadette,

147
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:18,180
drinking water -- carbonated as it was a special occasion.

148
00:07:18,590 --> 00:07:20,820
Penny's friend Zack stopped by and said hello

149
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:22,890
and I said "hoo."

150
00:07:23,750 --> 00:07:24,680
Who?

151
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:26,370
Zack.

152
00:07:26,370 --> 00:07:28,220
Then why did you ask?

153
00:07:28,650 --> 00:07:30,120
- Ask what?  - Who.

154
00:07:30,120 --> 00:07:31,750
Zack.

155
00:07:32,810 --> 00:07:34,610
All right, let's start over.

156
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,210
What did you say when Zack walked in?

157
00:07:37,210 --> 00:07:39,180
- "Hoo".  - Zack.

158
00:07:39,980 --> 00:07:41,440
Why do you keep saying "Zack"?

159
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:43,150
Because you keep saying "who."

160
00:07:43,150 --> 00:07:44,370
I'm not saying "hoo" now.

161
00:07:44,370 --> 00:07:45,890
I said "hoo" last night.

162
00:07:45,890 --> 00:07:48,690
And the answer was Zack, correct?

163
00:07:48,690 --> 00:07:49,780
There was no question.

164
00:07:49,780 --> 00:07:51,790
I simply said "hoo."

165
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:56,060
All right, I think I have enough to go on.

166
00:07:57,790 --> 00:08:00,190
Possible explanations for your symptoms are,

167
00:08:00,190 --> 00:08:02,200
in descending order of likelihood:

168
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:05,340
hyperthyroidism, premature menopause,

169
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:08,570
hosting an alien parasite or --

170
00:08:08,570 --> 00:08:10,410
and I only include it for the sake of covering

171
00:08:10,410 --> 00:08:14,150
absolutely all bases -- sexual arousal.

172
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:19,720
Where would I have picked up an alien parasite?

173
00:08:22,990 --> 00:08:24,270
There you are.

174
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:28,470
Let me ask you a question. What are you most afraid of?

175
00:08:28,470 --> 00:08:29,690
I don't know. Um...

176
00:08:29,690 --> 00:08:30,770
nuclear war.

177
00:08:30,770 --> 00:08:32,530
Accidentally being buried alive.

178
00:08:32,530 --> 00:08:34,150
Any of those movies where you get that phone call

179
00:08:34,150 --> 00:08:35,660
that says you're going to die.

180
00:08:35,660 --> 00:08:37,590
And then you do.

181
00:08:37,890 --> 00:08:39,430
No. Something very specific

182
00:08:39,430 --> 00:08:40,680
that we both know you,

183
00:08:40,680 --> 00:08:44,070
Rajesh Koothrappali, are terrified of.

184
00:08:44,070 --> 00:08:46,610
Well, type two diabetes runs in my family.

185
00:08:46,610 --> 00:08:47,470
The thought of losing a toe...

186
00:08:47,470 --> 00:08:50,120
Spiders! You're afraid of spiders!

187
00:08:51,180 --> 00:08:52,190
What the heck is this?

188
00:08:52,190 --> 00:08:54,770
A jar with a big spider in it, of course.

189
00:08:55,490 --> 00:08:56,480
Bravery test.

190
00:08:56,480 --> 00:08:59,470
First one to take his hand out is the sidekick.

191
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:03,310
Are you crazy?

192
00:09:03,310 --> 00:09:05,160
Perhaps.

193
00:09:06,360 --> 00:09:07,510
Are you scared?

194
00:09:07,510 --> 00:09:09,330
No. But it's a stupid test.

195
00:09:09,330 --> 00:09:12,530
Oh, really? What if the Earth was in danger

196
00:09:12,530 --> 00:09:13,640
and the only way to save it was

197
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:16,860
to stick your hand in a jar with a spider?

198
00:09:16,860 --> 00:09:18,050
Oh, yeah? What if the Earth was in danger

199
00:09:18,050 --> 00:09:19,760
and the only way to save it was to take a shower

200
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:22,790
in the locker room and let other guys see you naked?

201
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:26,980
Oh, come on. That's never gonna happen.

202
00:09:27,730 --> 00:09:29,280
Put your hand in the jar

203
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:32,600
or forever be revealed as my sidekick.

204
00:09:32,970 --> 00:09:35,540
All right, I will.

205
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:39,080
How did you get so brave all of a sudden?

206
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:40,370
It's easy.

207
00:09:40,370 --> 00:09:41,990
The spider's crawling up your arm.

208
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:49,640
Get it off! Get it off!

209
00:09:49,640 --> 00:09:51,290
Please, Raj!

210
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:55,510
Please.

211
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:03,290
My blood work shows thyroid function normal.

212
00:10:03,290 --> 00:10:05,050
Cortisol levels normal.

213
00:10:05,660 --> 00:10:08,580
How about your follicle-stimulating hormone levels?

214
00:10:08,580 --> 00:10:11,000
Sheldon, I am not going through menopause.

215
00:10:11,710 --> 00:10:12,830
Are you sure?

216
00:10:12,830 --> 00:10:16,160
You said that with the testy bark of an old biddy.

217
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:20,410
I think we need to face the cold, hard truth:

218
00:10:20,410 --> 00:10:23,740
I was sexually aroused by Penny's friend Zack.

219
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:25,240
Hang on. I don't know

220
00:10:25,240 --> 00:10:27,590
that we've given the alien parasite hypothesis

221
00:10:27,590 --> 00:10:29,400
a fair shake.

222
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:30,990
Let's look at this logically.

223
00:10:30,990 --> 00:10:33,020
I have a stomach; I get hungry.

224
00:10:33,020 --> 00:10:36,880
I have genitals; I have the potential for sexual arousal.

225
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:39,350
A cross we all must bear.

226
00:10:41,060 --> 00:10:44,240
You know, in difficult moments like this,

227
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:47,270
I often turn to a force greater than myself.

228
00:10:47,270 --> 00:10:48,040
Religion?

229
00:10:48,040 --> 00:10:49,570
Star Trek.

230
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:52,650
Did you see Star Trek: The Motion Picture?

231
00:10:52,650 --> 00:10:55,330
- No. Don't. - It's terrible. However...

232
00:10:55,330 --> 00:10:56,870
in it, we learn that

233
00:10:56,870 --> 00:11:00,050
when Spock finds himself drawn off the path of logic

234
00:11:00,050 --> 00:11:03,980
by feelings bubbling up from his human half, he suppresses them

235
00:11:03,980 --> 00:11:07,190
using the Vulcan mental discipline of Kolinahr.

236
00:11:07,190 --> 00:11:08,730
Are you suggesting we live our lives

237
00:11:08,730 --> 00:11:11,400
guided by the philosophies found in cheap science fiction?

238
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:13,730
"Cheap science fiction"?

239
00:11:18,890 --> 00:11:20,080
What are you doing?

240
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:25,800
Using Kolinar to suppress my anger at that last comment.

241
00:11:26,690 --> 00:11:28,830
Is it possible that your concern for me at this moment

242
00:11:28,830 --> 00:11:32,250
is motivated by nothing more than simple jealousy?

243
00:11:33,090 --> 00:11:35,280
I hadn't considered that.

244
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:37,440
Give me a moment.

245
00:11:39,630 --> 00:11:41,070
All right, I've considered it.

246
00:11:41,070 --> 00:11:43,570
- And? - I reject it.

247
00:11:44,210 --> 00:11:46,400
You reject it because you don't feel jealousy,

248
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:49,010
or because you are suppressing jealousy?

249
00:11:50,340 --> 00:11:52,720
I think I'll eat my lunch at home.

250
00:11:54,750 --> 00:11:55,900
That's not your lunch, Sheldon;

251
00:11:55,900 --> 00:11:58,280
those are the cadaver brain specimens.

252
00:12:01,810 --> 00:12:03,450
As they were incorrectly sliced,

253
00:12:03,450 --> 00:12:06,920
you can see how I could mistake them for my sashimi.

254
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:17,470
- Hello. - Hey.

255
00:12:17,470 --> 00:12:19,860
Isn't tomorrow your usual laundry night?

256
00:12:19,860 --> 00:12:23,170
The supermarket was out of my regular fabric softener.

257
00:12:23,170 --> 00:12:25,610
If this one under-or over-softens,

258
00:12:25,610 --> 00:12:27,940
I'll need time to make things right.

259
00:12:28,950 --> 00:12:30,370
That's thinking ahead.

260
00:12:30,370 --> 00:12:33,560
The alternative would be to think backwards.

261
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:35,960
That's just remembering.

262
00:12:37,250 --> 00:12:38,480
So how's Amy?

263
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:40,670
Amy's changed.

264
00:12:40,670 --> 00:12:42,900
I might have to let her go.

265
00:12:43,370 --> 00:12:44,800
Oh, no. Why?

266
00:12:44,800 --> 00:12:47,300
I thought she was a highly evolved creature

267
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:49,760
of pure intellect, like me.

268
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:51,120
But recent events indicate that

269
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:54,160
she may be a slave to her baser urges.

